  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "Stark drew his assumed growth rate from documented rates of twentieth-century Mormon expansion.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "Stark estimated that the Christian population of the Roman Empire grew at roughly 40 percent per decade.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "Stark's model places fewer than one million Christians in the empire in 250 CE.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "Stark's model places more than one million Christians in the empire in 250 CE.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "A review of ancient demographic estimates determined that Stark's projection yields more than one million Christians in the empire by 250 CE.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "Stark maintained that conversion followed attachment to believers rather than prior persuasion by doctrine.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "pos": 7,
      "text": "In Stark's account, people formed social bonds with Christians first and accepted Christian teaching afterward.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "pos": 8,
      "text": "Early Christian recruitment followed pre-existing ties of household, kinship, and trade.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "pos": 9,
      "text": "Christian teaching offered assurances of personal immortality unavailable in the civic cults.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "pos": 10,
      "text": "Friedrich Engels described early Christianity as a movement of slaves and the dispossessed.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "pos": 11,
      "text": "Meeks reconstructed the social status of converts from the names and descriptions preserved in the Pauline letters.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "pos": 12,
      "text": "A review panel convened by the Society of Biblical Literature concluded that converts in the Pauline churches were drawn disproportionately from privileged urban strata rather than from slaves and the destitute.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "pos": 13,
      "text": "Harnack held that early Christian converts came exclusively from the senatorial aristocracy.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "pos": 14,
      "text": "Weber denied that religious ideas bore any affinity to the class position of their carriers.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    }
