  "response": "What settles a dispute of this kind is not the elegance of either explanation but the survival of one under evidence that could have falsified it: a defensible curve of increase, a reconstructed social profile, and a documented mechanism of recruitment. Where the profile and the mechanism converge while the doctrinal argument rests on inference from the content of the message alone, the sociological account carries the case for the period it covers, without denying that the content had to be worth accepting once the relationship had done its work.\n\nFriedrich Engels described early Christianity as a movement of slaves and the dispossessed. Meeks reconstructed the social status of converts from the names and descriptions preserved in the Pauline letters, and that reconstruction is the evidence on which a review panel convened by the Society of Biblical Literature concluded that converts in the Pauline churches were drawn disproportionately from privileged urban strata rather than from slaves and the destitute. Engels's portrait and the panel's conclusion cannot both be true: a membership concentrated in the comfortable strata of the cities rules out a movement composed of the enslaved and the destitute. The panel's verdict stands even though Harnack held that early Christian converts came exclusively from the senatorial aristocracy. Whereas Engels anchored the movement in the lowest ranks of imperial society, Weber denied that religious ideas bore any affinity to the class position of their carriers.\n\nStark maintained that conversion followed attachment to believers rather than prior persuasion by doctrine; in other words, in his account people formed social bonds with Christians first and accepted Christian teaching afterward. Weighing such a priority claim requires asking both where adherents came from and what the message supplied to them. At least one of the following holds of the movement's expansion, and perhaps both do: early Christian recruitment followed pre-existing ties of household, kinship, and trade, and Christian teaching offered assurances of personal immortality unavailable in the civic cults.\n\nStark drew his assumed growth rate from documented rates of twentieth-century Mormon expansion, and that documented provenance confirms his estimate that the Christian population of the Roman Empire grew at roughly 40 percent per decade. Debate has since centred on what Stark's projection implies for the mid-third century. On that question his model admits only two possibilities, exactly one of which must be correct: either Stark's model places fewer than one million Christians in the empire in 250 CE, or it places more than one million Christians in the empire in that year. A review of ancient demographic estimates determined that Stark's projection yields more than one million Christians in the empire by 250 CE, and that determination shows that the larger of the two outcomes is the one his model in fact generates.\n\nScholars adjudicate between doctrinal and sociological accounts of a movement's expansion by converting each into claims that independent evidence can test. A doctrinal account predicts that teaching precedes affiliation and that adherents cluster wherever the message meets a felt deficiency in the available alternatives; a sociological account predicts that affiliation precedes belief and that adherents cluster wherever pre-existing relationships reach. The relevant material is therefore of three kinds: quantitative modelling of expansion, prosopographical reconstruction of who actually joined, and documentation of the pathways along which recruitment travelled.",
